.TH DRYDOCK-MACHO-REWRITE 1 "2026-09-29" "Drydock" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
drydock-macho-rewrite \- rewrite and inspect Mach-O executables
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B drydock-macho-rewrite
.I input output
.br
.B drydock-macho-rewrite
.RI "verify FILE | info [--thin] FILE | imports FILE | exports FILE"
.SH DESCRIPTION
Given a script on standard input, drydock-macho-rewrite writes a modified
copy of a Mach-O executable. The query verbs inspect a file without
changing it.
.SS Script format
One statement per line.
Blank lines are ignored.
Begin a comment with \fB#\fR at the start of an unquoted field.
Fields are split like shell words:
.UL
.IP \[bu] 2
Whitespace-separated
.IP \[bu] 2
Single- and double-quote grouping
.IP \[bu] 2
Backslash escapes
.SS Statements
.IP
.nf
\f(CW
load-command  delete    KIND        uuid | codesig | source-version
                                    | build-version | code-sign-drs
segment       rename    OLD NEW
minos         at-most   VERSION     e.g. 10.9, 10.12, 10.9.5
minos         if-absent VERSION
swift-abi     set       legacy
fixups        set       classic
dylib         replace   OLD NEW
dylib         append    PATH
dylib         insert    PATH
dylib         delete    PATH
dylib         reexport  PATH
dylib         retype    PATH KIND   load | weak | reexport | upward
rpath         replace   OLD NEW
rpath         delete    PATH
rpath         append    PATH
rpath         insert    PATH
import        redirect  SYMBOL FROM-LIB TO-LIB
target        10.9                  the one statement whose meaning depends on
                                    the binary; see \[dq]The \[ga]target\[ga] statement\[dq]
\fR
.fi
.PP
Statements run one at a time, in the order written, so
\fBdylib insert A\fR then \fBdylib insert B\fR
leaves B at ordinal 1 and A at ordinal 2.
Likewise for \fBrpath insert\fR, where dyld will search B before A.
.PP
\fBminos at-most VERSION\fR lowers a declared minimum.
\fBminos if-absent VERSION\fR leaves any declared minimum as it is.
Both declare VERSION where nothing is declared, and both compare at
VERSION\[aq]s own precision, so \fBminos at-most 10.9\fR keeps a
declared 10.9.5.
A minimum read from an \fBLC_BUILD_VERSION\fR keeps that
command\[aq]s sdk; with nothing declared, the sdk written is 10.9.
.PP
Finder and LaunchServices refuse to launch on
\fBLSMinimumSystemVersion\fR in \fBInfo.plist\fR, not on the
Mach-O minimum.
On 10.9 the sdk field decides linked-on-or-after behaviour and whether
dyld registers a dylib\[aq]s code signature; see
docs/minimum-os-version.md.
Each \fBminos\fR statement leaves exactly one
\fBLC_VERSION_MIN_MACOSX\fR per slice, and no
\fBLC_BUILD_VERSION\fR.
On a universal binary, write \fBarch x86_64\fR to leave the other
slices\[aq] minimums alone.
.SS Re-signing
Every edit invalidates a code signature, and known port flows re-sign ad
hoc afterwards (\fBcodesign --force --sign -\fR).
Sign last, after the last \fBdrydock-macho-rewrite\fR run.
10.9\[aq]s own \fBcodesign\fR can re-sign what Drydock writes,
because a run that changes anything in \fB__LINKEDIT\fR also puts
\fB__LINKEDIT\fR back in the order 10.9\[aq]s
\fBcodesign_allocate\fR requires.
The report says so:
.IP
.nf
\f(CW
Mantle: __LINKEDIT re-packed in codesign_allocate\[aq]s order: 40,992 -> 38,176 bytes, 2,828 unreferenced bytes dropped
\fR
.fi
.PP
\fBinfo\fR\[aq]s last line says whether 10.9\[aq]s
\fBcodesign_allocate\fR would re-sign the file as it stands:
.IP
.nf
\f(CW
resign 10.9: ok
resign 10.9: malformed object (unknown load command 4)
resign 10.9: slice x86_64h: malformed object (unknown load command 4)
\fR
.fi
.PP
A file that \fBcodesign_allocate\fR would accept but re-sign
\f[B]corrupt\fR, with its fixup opcodes or symbol table overwritten
under a signature that \fBcodesign -v\fR still accepts, gets a
second line, \fBresign corrupt: \&...\fR.
No run writes such a file: one it cannot repair is refused.
.PP
\fBload-command delete codesig\fR removes the signature\[aq]s bytes,
not only its load command.
A universal binary is checked slice by slice, and one slice 10.9 cannot
re-sign stops the whole file; on 10.9, which runs only the x86_64 slice,
\fBlipo -thin x86_64\fR first.
.SS Directives
.IP
.nf
\f(CW
arch NAME           apply the script only to the slice named NAME (lipo\[aq]s
                    names: x86_64, x86_64h, arm64, arm64e, i386); repeatable.
                    Without it, every 64-bit slice of a fat file is edited
allow-unmatched     an operation that matched nothing is reported and the run
                    continues, instead of refusing the whole run
\fR
.fi
.PP
If used, these must appear before any other operations.
.PP
An operation that matches nothing refuses the whole run (exit 1, nothing
written).
These are the statements that can match nothing:
.IP \[bu] 2
\fBload-command delete\fR (no command of that kind)
.IP \[bu] 2
\fBdylib replace/delete/reexport/retype\fR and
\fBrpath replace/delete\fR (no command naming that path)
.IP \[bu] 2
\fBsegment rename\fR (no segment of that name)
.IP \[bu] 2
\fBimport redirect\fR (no bind of that symbol names that library)
.PP
On a fat file, a statement has matched if it matched in any selected
slice.
.PP
\fBminos\fR and \fBswift-abi set\fR cannot miss: with nothing to
do, they are no-ops.
\fBswift-abi set legacy\fR refuses an image that still has chained
fixups, whose class-record pointers it cannot read: write
\fBfixups set classic\fR before it.
\fBfixups set classic\fR is a no-op on an image that already uses
\fBLC_DYLD_INFO_ONLY\fR, but it refuses an image with neither that
nor chained fixups.
On a chained image it also refuses when there is no single macOS
\fBLC_BUILD_VERSION\fR to keep as a version-min.
That means two macOS ones, or one for a platform other than macOS (such
as iOS or a simulator), alone or beside macOS.
\fBallow-unmatched\fR covers none of these refusals.
.PP
\fBallow-unmatched\fR is for the wrappers that reproduce tools which
exited 0 when an operation matched nothing: \fBchange_dylib\fR,
\fBfix_macho\fR and \fBinsert_dylib\fR head their scripts with
it.
compat/README.md records why for each.
.SS The \fBtarget\fR statement
.IP
.nf
\f(CW
target        10.9
\fR
.fi
.PP
Every other statement means the same thing for every input \[em]
\fBdylib replace A B\fR may match nothing, but \f[I]what it asks
for\fR is fixed.
\fBtarget 10.9\fR asks a different question of every binary and
answers it differently.
.PP
\f[B]It expands, where it is written, into statements the language
already has\fR \[em] the ones this binary actually needs \[em] and
those run in its place.
It is this script, with each conditional line kept only where its
condition holds:
.IP
.nf
\f(CW
fixups set classic                   # where LC_DYLD_CHAINED_FIXUPS is present
minos at-most 10.9
segment rename __DATA_CONST __DATA   # where __DATA_CONST holds __objc_ sections
swift-abi set legacy                 # where class records carry the stable-ABI Swift tag
\fR
.fi
.PP
Each condition is tested on the image as the lines before it left it, so
the Swift tag is read after \fBfixups set classic\fR has made the
class-record pointers readable.
.PP
Each detection is exact rather than a guess: a load command is present
or it is not, a section name begins with \fB__objc_\fR or it does
not, a tag bit is set or it is not.
\f[B]Never \fBdylib\f[B] or \fBrpath\f[B] work\fR \[em] no tool
can guess which stub dylib you meant, and that is the dominant real
workload, so a profile stops where the guessing would start.
.PP
What \fBtarget\fR adds over writing that script by hand:
.IP \[bu] 2
it renames \fB__DATA_CONST\fR only where \fB__objc_\fR sections
need it, per slice.
Renaming a C-only \fB__DATA_CONST\fR breaks nothing measured on
10.9, but it leaves two segments named \fB__DATA\fR, which
\fBgetsegbyname\fR and tools cannot tell apart;
.IP \[bu] 2
it chooses per slice of a fat file;
.IP \[bu] 2
it derives \fBfixups set classic\fR only where there are chained
fixups, so it never hits that statement\[aq]s refusal on an image with
no fixup information;
.IP \[bu] 2
it reports why each line was derived.
.PP
A second profile is what would show the design earns its place; this
build has one, and refuses any other.
.RS
.PP
A declared minimum at or below 10.9 (every 10.9.x counts as 10.9) is
left as it is: raising it would discard a true fact and buy nothing.
10.9\[aq]s dyld does not enforce the field \[em] a 10.12 executable
runs, and a 10.12 dylib loads \[em] so lowering it keeps the file honest
rather than making it load.
.RE
.PP
\f[B]Use \fBtarget 10.9\f[B]\fR to make a whole binary built for a
newer macOS run on 10.9, especially one with chained fixups or more than
one slice.
It combines with the \fBdylib\fR and \fBrpath\fR lines it never
derives, as below.
.PP
\f[B]Write the statements by hand\fR when you want some of those
changes and not the rest.
Then:
.IP \[bu] 2
put \fBfixups set classic\fR first, and leave it out for an image
with neither chained fixups nor \fBLC_DYLD_INFO_ONLY\fR (it
refuses);
.IP \[bu] 2
put \fBswift-abi set legacy\fR after it (it refuses an image that
still has chained fixups);
.IP \[bu] 2
rename \fB__DATA_CONST\fR only where \fBinfo\fR shows
\fB__objc_\fR sections in it;
.IP \[bu] 2
when slices need different lines, run one script per slice with
\fBarch\fR, since a directive applies to the whole script.
.PP
\fBinfo\fR shows what \fBtarget\fR would act on: each of its
detections is an \fBinfo\fR line (see Queries).
.PP
\f[B]Position is not cosmetic, which is why this is a statement and not
a flag.\fR \fBfixups set classic\fR rewrites \fB__LINKEDIT\fR
and strips load commands, which changes the header pad available to
every \fBdylib replace\fR after it, and nothing reorders your
statements \[em] the script is the plan.
Where you write the line is where the expansion lands:
.IP
.nf
\f(CW
target 10.9

dylib replace /System/Library/Frameworks/Metal.framework/Versions/A/Metal  \[at]loader_path/libMetalStub.dylib
rpath  insert  \[at]loader_path/../Frameworks
\fR
.fi
.PP
The report lists the expansion line by line, with the finding that
produced each, since the same line does different things to different
binaries:
.IP
.nf
\f(CW
  target 10.9
    fixups set classic  (LC_DYLD_CHAINED_FIXUPS present)
      chained fixups -> LC_DYLD_INFO_ONLY; LC_BUILD_VERSION 12.0 (sdk 12.3) kept as LC_VERSION_MIN_MACOSX
    minos at-most 10.9  (always)
      version-min 12.0 -> 10.9; sdk 12.3 kept
\fR
.fi
.RS
.PP
Each statement\[aq]s own lines follow it; the conversion\[aq]s other
figures are left out here.
The report says
\fBnothing to do: this binary already targets 10.9\fR only when no
line changed the image: the slice already held one
\fBLC_VERSION_MIN_MACOSX\fR at or below 10.9, no
\fBLC_BUILD_VERSION\fR, and nothing else to convert.
.RE
.PP
The rest of the rules:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]One \fBtarget\f[B] per script.\fR A second is a parse error.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]An unknown target is a refusal.\fR \fBtarget 10.10\fR errors,
naming what this build does know, rather than silently doing 10.9\[aq]s
work.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]A derived statement behaves as the one you would have written\fR
\[em] a derived \fBminos at-most 10.9\fR grows a short header pad
exactly as one you wrote does \[em] and if a derived statement is
refused, the refusal names the \fBtarget\fR line, which is the line
you wrote.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\fBtarget\f[B] never counts as unmatched,\fR and neither does
anything it derived: \[dq]this binary already targets 10.9
correctly\[dq] is a correct answer for a profile, unlike for an explicit
operation.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Writing \fBtarget 10.9\f[B] \fBand\f[B], after it, an
explicit statement it derived makes the explicit one redundant, and if
that statement can miss, it refuses as unmatched.\fR That is right,
and is documented rather than special-cased.
.SS Growing the header
A statement that needs more header pad than the image has makes room
first, and says so:
.IP
.nf
\f(CW
Mantle: grew the header pad by 4096 bytes (16 -> 4112 available); contents raised by 0x1000; new UUID
\fR
.fi
.PP
An executable grows by lowering its image base into \fB__PAGEZERO\fR,
so no address that names content changes.
A dylib or bundle has no \fB__PAGEZERO\fR, so it grows by a
\f[B]raise\fR: every address in it moves up by whole pages, and so does
every pointer that holds one.
Besides that, a raise:
.IP \[bu] 2
gives the image a new \fBLC_UUID\fR, derived from the old one, so that a
dSYM for the old addresses is not taken for this image\[aq]s;
.IP \[bu] 2
drops \fBLC_SEGMENT_SPLIT_INFO\fR, whose positions would be stale (the
report ends \fBdropped LC_SEGMENT_SPLIT_INFO\fR);
.IP \[bu] 2
invalidates the code signature, as every edit does: re-sign last.
.PP
A raise refuses, writing nothing, an image that:
.IP \[bu] 2
is not x86_64, since its code is decoded to find what addresses the
header;
.IP \[bu] 2
has no \fBLC_DYLD_INFO\fR or \fBLC_DYLD_INFO_ONLY\fR, since only its
rebase opcodes list every pointer that moves, or has a
\fBLC_DYSYMTAB\fR with table-of-contents, module or relocation entries
beside it;
.IP \[bu] 2
has an \fBLC_UNIXTHREAD\fR or \fBLC_THREAD\fR, or a section aligned to
more than a page;
.IP \[bu] 2
has a rebase or bind in the segment that maps the header, or a pointer,
symbol, export or RIP-relative reference naming the bytes between the
header and the first content, which a raise moves apart;
.IP \[bu] 2
has a segment other than the one that maps the header whose file data
starts before the first content, or a section that lies below it;
.IP \[bu] 2
has a stab of a type a raise does not know how to move;
.IP \[bu] 2
has code that addresses the image\[aq]s own header and that the grow
cannot repair or confirm; or
.IP \[bu] 2
has a function-starts leading delta that would need a wider encoding
once raised.
.PP
Both routes also refuse an image that:
.IP \[bu] 2
has chained fixups: run \fBfixups set classic\fR first;
.IP \[bu] 2
is encrypted (a nonzero \fBcryptid\fR) or has a segment flagged
\fBSG_PROTECTED_VERSION_1\fR; or
.IP \[bu] 2
has a load command the grow cannot classify, or
\fBLC_LINKER_OPTIMIZATION_HINT\fR, \fBLC_NOTE\fR or \fBLC_ATOM_INFO\fR.
.PP
An executable, which lowers its base, also refuses an image that has
\fBLC_SEGMENT_SPLIT_INFO\fR (a raise drops it), is not PIE, is arm64
(whose pages are 16 KB), or has a \fB__PAGEZERO\fR too small to give the
header what it needs.
.PP
Every grow checks its own result and refuses, writing nothing, on any
difference.
It checks that:
.IP \[bu] 2
every address, section, symbol, rebase target and file offset is its old
value moved by exactly the raise, or unchanged where it named the header;
.IP \[bu] 2
every byte past the header is the old one moved, and each load command
is byte-identical except for the fields above;
.IP \[bu] 2
structures that were computed separately agree: initializers are
function starts, and each export\[aq]s address equals its symbol\[aq]s;
and
.IP \[bu] 2
no code still addresses the header unrepaired, and no two segments
overlap.
.PP
Three limits of that:
.IP \[bu] 2
Grows add up.
The function-starts leading delta gains the raise at each grow, so an
image grown more than once can reach the refusal above.
.IP \[bu] 2
Code is decoded to find a reference to the header.
A real reference that follows a jump table in \fB__text\fR not declared
by \fBLC_DATA_IN_CODE\fR can be taken for data, and the grow then passes
it unrepaired.
.IP \[bu] 2
A stab counts as named when its name is a non-empty string.
An unnamed \fBN_FUN\fR holds a size, which a raise does not move; one
whose \fBn_strx\fR happens to name a non-empty string is taken for named,
and its value is moved.
.SS Queries
Four verbs read a file and change nothing:
.IP
.nf
\f(CW
drydock-macho-rewrite verify FILE          is the file structurally plausible?
drydock-macho-rewrite info [--thin] FILE   load commands, ordinals, sections, header pad
drydock-macho-rewrite imports FILE         TSV, one row per bind
drydock-macho-rewrite exports FILE         TSV, one row per export
\fR
.fi
.PP
\fBinfo\fR reads a fat container as one block per 64-bit slice, and
names the slices it passes over; \fB--thin\fR refuses a fat
container instead (exit 1).
\fBverify\fR reads only a thin file.
\fBimports\fR and \fBexports\fR read either; each row names its
slice in an \fBarch\fR column, and the header row names the columns.
.PP
\fBverify\fR checks structure, not what 10.9\[aq]s runtime requires:
it passes an Objective-C image whose \fB__objc_\fR sections sit in
\fB__DATA_CONST\fR, and that image dies at launch on 10.9.
.PP
\fBinfo\fR answers each of \fBtarget 10.9\fR\[aq]s three
detections: an \fBLC[n]\fR line for
\fBLC_DYLD_CHAINED_FIXUPS\fR, a \fBsectname=\fR line under
\fBsegname=__DATA_CONST\fR, and the \fBswift-abi:\fR line, which
says \fBunknown\fR on an image whose fixups are still chained.
.RS
.PP
The declared minimum is on the line beneath its command:
\fBversion=\&... sdk=\&...\fR under \fBLC_VERSION_MIN_MACOSX\fR,
\fBplatform=\&... minos=\&... sdk=\&...\fR under
\fBLC_BUILD_VERSION\fR.
.RE
.PP
From \fBdrydock-macho-rewrite info hello\fR, on a two-slice x86_64 +
i386 build:
.IP
.nf
\f(CW
hello: 16576 bytes, 2 slices
slice x86_64: 4312 bytes, 15 load commands, filetype=2
LC[0] LC_SEGMENT_64 cmdsize=72
  segname=__PAGEZERO vmaddr=0x0 vmsize=0x100000000 fileoff=0 filesize=0 nsects=0
LC[1] LC_SEGMENT_64 cmdsize=312
  segname=__TEXT vmaddr=0x100000000 vmsize=0x1000 fileoff=0 filesize=4096 nsects=3
    sectname=__text
    sectname=__unwind_info
    sectname=__eh_frame
\&...
LC[8] LC_VERSION_MIN_MACOSX cmdsize=16
  version=10.9.0 sdk=10.9.0
\&...
LC[11] LC_LOAD_DYLIB cmdsize=56
  ordinal=1 path=/usr/lib/libSystem.B.dylib
\&...
swift-abi: no class records carry the stable-ABI tag
header pad: 3112 bytes available (LC end=856, first sect=3968)
slice i386: 32-bit; passed through unchanged
resign 10.9: not checked: slice i386 is not a 64-bit Mach-O
\fR
.fi
.PP
On a thin file there is no slice header: the first line names the path.
.SS Limitations
.IP \[bu] 2
32-bit input is currently refused.
.IP \[bu] 2
64-bit fat containers (\fBfat_arch_64\fR) are currently refused.
.IP \[bu] 2
A raise needs an x86_64 dylib or bundle with compressed dyld info; see
Growing the header.
